WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.940
In the recent film starting Tom Holland,

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.270
Peter Parker cooks up his own webbing

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:07.710
in his high-school chemistry class.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.540
Now, he could have made
it out of anything,

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.170
like fishing line or even steel,

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:17.060
and yet he chooses to
replicate spider silk.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.810
Spider silk.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:23.690
This kid is trusting his life
to a flimsy-looking strand

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.290
of arachnid goo.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.340
But it turns out, if Peter's web

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.920
is anything like real spider silk,

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.930
then his web-slinging
antics are more realistic

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.153
than they might appear.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.900
Now, spider silk doesn't
look very durable.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.850
After all, a strand can
be as little as 1/40

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.860
the thickness of a human hair,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.790
but, pound for pound,
it's stronger than steel.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:54.090
So if you twisted spider
silk into a thread

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.400
that was 2 millimeters wide,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.210
as thick as a strand of spaghetti,

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:02.040
it could support 900
pounds before breaking,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.990
strong enough for a
polar bear to hang from.

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:09.030
So a scrawny kid, like
Spider-Man? He's got this.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:09.980
Jim Kakalios: And that's just for a

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.670
2-millimeter-diameter webbing.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:16.088
If he needs more, he just
makes it a little bit thicker,

00:01:16.088 --> 00:01:18.010
and it could support even more weight.

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:20.530
Narrator: That's physicist Jim Kakalios,

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:23.700
the author of "The
Physics of Superheroes."

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.743
He says that the secret
to spider silk's strength

00:01:27.743 --> 00:01:29.670
is its structure.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.430
Real spider silk has two major components:

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:37.570
extremely rigid nanocrystals
that make the silk sturdy

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:41.200
and stretchy, elastic
polymers that make it pliable.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.430
That combination of tough and flexible

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.530
makes the silk extremely hard to tear,

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.880
and, if you look at Peter's lab notes,

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.950
it looks like he tries to
mimic that same structure.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.900
Kakalios: So, it looks like it
is a set of organic molecules

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.650
that he is using,

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:02.890
and he's trying to combine them in ways

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.248
to polymerize them, to basically

00:02:05.248 --> 00:02:07.506
take these complex molecules

00:02:07.506 --> 00:02:10.840
and link them together in longer chains

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.360
that would then presumably fold down

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.430
and develop these nanocrystals

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.170
and the elastic polymers.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.690
Narrator: But Peter may
have gone one step further

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:25.372
and actually made one improvement
to his synthetic silk.

00:02:25.372 --> 00:02:28.180
Kakalios: I think that, instead
of these little nanocrystals

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.900
of proteins that spiders use,

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.060
he might be using carbon nanotubes

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:34.870
to provide the strength and rigidity.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:36.190
Narrator: Carbon nanotubes

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.400
are basically a sheet of carbon atoms

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.380
that's been rolled up into a tube.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:44.280
And if Jim's right, Peter is
one smart high-school student,

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.990
because these tiny tubes

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.580
are actually some of
the strongest material

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.260
known to humans.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:54.573
In fact, they're over 100
times as strong as steel.

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:58.370
And that's when they're microscopic.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.810
So, a spaghetti-thin strand of this stuff,

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.170
like what we see in "Spider-Man"?

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:06.962
It could support far more
than just 900 pounds.

00:03:06.962 --> 00:03:08.428
Kakalios: That would be able to support

00:03:08.428 --> 00:03:11.320
over 40,000 pounds.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.430
Narrator: Suddenly that ferry scene

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:15.540
doesn't seem so far-fetched,

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.140
especially since we have the technology

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.590
to make those nanotubes in real life.

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:24.960
Scientists at the University
of Cincinnati, for example,

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.520
have figured out how to grow
carbon nanotubes in a lab

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.830
and then spool them into threads.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:32.600
Sadly, those threads

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.610
aren't meant for
skyscraper-swinging antics.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:39.750
The researcher's goal
is a tad more practical.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.218
Kakalios: If you could manufacture it

00:03:41.218 --> 00:03:43.500
and make threads out of carbon nanotubes,

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:44.900
you could make lightweight clothing

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:46.590
that would be stronger than Kevlar.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.650
Narrator: So, when you
really think about it,

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.260
the most unrealistic thing

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:52.846
about Peter Parker's homemade webbing

00:03:52.846 --> 00:03:54.930
is that a high schooler
figured out how to make it

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.580
in his chem class.

